N2  - The increased utilization of small satellites, particularly CubeSats, has been propelled by technological advancements and the widespread incorporation of Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) components. This transition has ushered in a paradigm of faster and more cost- effective missions, giving rise to novel trends such as the Internet of Space Things (IoST) and Space as a Service (SaaS) in the space domain. However, this shift comes with a concomitant rise in spacecraft architecture complexity, expanding the attack surface for potential cyber threats. Despite these advancements, space cybersecurity has lagged behind, largely adhering to traditional security-through-obscurity approaches, thereby endangering the security of future missions and critical infrastructure. This work delves into the OPS-SAT mission as a case study, presenting one of the most advanced CubeSat missions to date. OPS-SAT introduces innovative mission capabilities and novel concepts in space operations. In this study, we assume the role of malicious users, crafting and executing a cyber attack against the primary payload of the OPS-SAT spacecraft, within an emulated environment. The success of the attack is scrutinized, and the resultant impacts across the system are systematically enumerated. The study culminates in a formalized representation of the attack using established threat modeling frameworks, including attack trees and the SPARTA matrix. This approach offers a comprehensive examination of the OPS-SAT mission?s susceptibility to cyber threats, providing insights that can inform future cybersecurity strategies for advanced small satellite missions.
AB  - The increased utilization of small satellites, particularly CubeSats, has been propelled by technological advancements and the widespread incorporation of Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) components. This transition has ushered in a paradigm of faster and more cost- effective missions, giving rise to novel trends such as the Internet of Space Things (IoST) and Space as a Service (SaaS) in the space domain. However, this shift comes with a concomitant rise in spacecraft architecture complexity, expanding the attack surface for potential cyber threats. Despite these advancements, space cybersecurity has lagged behind, largely adhering to traditional security-through-obscurity approaches, thereby endangering the security of future missions and critical infrastructure. This work delves into the OPS-SAT mission as a case study, presenting one of the most advanced CubeSat missions to date. OPS-SAT introduces innovative mission capabilities and novel concepts in space operations. In this study, we assume the role of malicious users, crafting and executing a cyber attack against the primary payload of the OPS-SAT spacecraft, within an emulated environment. The success of the attack is scrutinized, and the resultant impacts across the system are systematically enumerated. The study culminates in a formalized representation of the attack using established threat modeling frameworks, including attack trees and the SPARTA matrix. This approach offers a comprehensive examination of the OPS-SAT mission?s susceptibility to cyber threats, providing insights that can inform future cybersecurity strategies for advanced small satellite missions.
